	"notes": "BBC News is a good example of a page that genuinely gets WIDER content on a bigger screen rather than just wider margins — the lead story and the four-card row underneath both stretch to fill a 3440 monitor, unlike several other sites in this set that cap themselves and centre. The only part of the page that behaves like those other sites is the footer, which stays a fixed 1280px and centres itself no matter how wide the window gets. The top story block is a classic narrow-wide-narrow shape: a text headline, a big photo story, and a second story, side by side; on a phone all of that becomes a single stacked column, photo first. One thing worth knowing: the big empty grey box under the navigation in every desktop screenshot is not a mistake — the scan shows it is a real advertisement slot that simply had no ad to show a headless browser.",
